WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
Elena owns universal life coverage and wants to reduce her payments

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:08.480
while changing jobs. Universal life separates premium funding,

3
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.320
interest credits, and policy deductions.

4
00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:17.180
The owner may have flexibility within the contract, but insurance costs

5
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.360
and administrative charges continue.

6
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.100
A planned premium is not automatically the amount guaranteed to sustain

7
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:28.960
coverage forever. If payments and earnings do not support the deductions,

8
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.540
the available value can decline.

9
00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:36.660
Without an applicable protection provision, insufficient funding can lead to

10
00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.260
lapse after the required process.

11
00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:45.200
First read the policy's funding conditions and current statement, then

12
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.260
discuss the effect of the proposed change.

13
00:00:48.282 --> 00:00:51.002
Use a deliberately simplified teaching account.

14
00:00:51.862 --> 00:00:53.702
It begins with one thousand dollars.

15
00:00:54.762 --> 00:00:59.882
A two-hundred-dollar payment adds one hundred ninety dollars after an assumed

16
00:00:59.882 --> 00:01:04.982
ten-dollar premium charge. Assume twenty dollars of interest is credited

17
00:01:04.982 --> 00:01:08.602
and one hundred thirty dollars of insurance and other charges are

18
00:01:08.602 --> 00:01:12.382
deducted. The ending value is one thousand eighty dollars.

19
00:01:13.082 --> 00:01:16.882
Now remove the payment while keeping the other assumptions fixed.

20
00:01:17.212 --> 00:01:20.162
The ending value becomes eight hundred ninety dollars.

21
00:01:21.372 --> 00:01:26.462
This example illustrates separate flows, not a real carrier's

22
00:01:26.462 --> 00:01:30.462
calculation order or guaranteed monthly return.

23
00:01:31.522 --> 00:01:35.342
Missing a payment does not make the insurance charges disappear.

24
00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.640
Interest-sensitive and current-assumption designs respond to the pricing and

25
00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:42.480
crediting terms stated in the contract.

26
00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:47.240
An indeterminate-premium whole life design may change its current premium

27
00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:48.780
within a guaranteed maximum.

28
00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:54.080
Universal life instead presents flexible funding and separately identified

29
00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.340
charges and credits. Do not treat those structures as identical.

30
00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:02.960
The insurer's current credited rate or illustrated performance may differ

31
00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:04.400
from the contractual minimum.

32
00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:09.400
Rising insurance costs or lower earnings can affect the funding needed.

33
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:14.800
A no-lapse guarantee, when included, has its own required payment pattern,

34
00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:20.140
period, and conditions. The word guarantee is useful only

35
00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:24.140
when you identify what is guaranteed and what must be done.

36
00:02:24.774 --> 00:02:30.294
Indexed life links a credited-interest calculation to a named external

37
00:02:30.294 --> 00:02:32.934
index under the policy's rules.

38
00:02:33.954 --> 00:02:38.814
An indexed universal life policy does not directly invest the owner's

39
00:02:38.814 --> 00:02:41.134
policy value in that stock index.

40
00:02:41.634 --> 00:02:46.654
A participation rate determines the share of the stated index change

41
00:02:46.654 --> 00:02:51.754
used. A cap limits the credited rate, while a spread can

42
00:02:51.754 --> 00:02:54.734
reduce the rate under a different stated formula.

43
00:02:55.674 --> 00:02:59.294
A floor provides the stated minimum for that crediting calculation.

44
00:03:00.574 --> 00:03:04.074
Methods and available rates differ by contract and strategy.

45
00:03:04.854 --> 00:03:08.754
Do not present the index's headline return as the policy's actual

46
00:03:08.754 --> 00:03:12.574
return or assume that stock dividends are included.

47
00:03:13.488 --> 00:03:19.148
Assume a teaching strategy uses an eight percent positive index change,

48
00:03:19.388 --> 00:03:23.928
seventy-five percent participation, a five percent cap,

49
00:03:24.428 --> 00:03:27.308
and a zero percent floor, with no spread.

50
00:03:28.368 --> 00:03:30.768
Participation produces six percent.

51
00:03:30.768 --> 00:03:34.428
The cap reduces the credited rate to five percent.

52
00:03:34.428 --> 00:03:39.508
Under the same strategy, a negative index change would produce

53
00:03:39.508 --> 00:03:42.988
zero percent interest because of the floor.

54
00:03:44.008 --> 00:03:47.648
These are illustrative assumptions, not currently offered rates.

55
00:03:48.528 --> 00:03:50.288
Policy charges still apply.

56
00:03:51.308 --> 00:03:55.548
Zero index interest can accompany a falling policy value after deductions.

57
00:03:56.468 --> 00:03:59.428
A crediting floor does not promise that the owner's net cash

58
00:03:59.428 --> 00:04:00.808
value can never decline.

59
00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:06.590
Elena should review the death-benefit option, current account and surrender

60
00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:11.810
values, deductions, premium history, and any guarantee's

61
00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:16.750
status. Some universal designs offer a level benefit,

62
00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:20.990
while others add the account value to a stated amount.

63
00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:26.330
Use the actual option and policy rules; do not assume every

64
00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:28.030
policy pays both amounts.

65
00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:32.690
Benefit increases may require new evidence of insurability.

66
00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:39.190
Request an updated in-force illustration using appropriate assumptions when

67
00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:44.950
reviewing future funding. Explain which figures are guaranteed and

68
00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:49.510
which are projections. A sound review connects the client's continuing

69
00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:55.690
protection need to an affordable funding plan
and the contract's actual conditions.
